# Navigation ownership

Read `docs/technical/navigation.md` before adding or moving a screen. On iOS phone the `(app)` stack is the only owner of temporary screens above the cockpit. `MenuSheet` presents the cockpit sheet once. Do not add another native modal owner.

Before writing the screen, name its navigator, who presents it, and what enter and back do. Reuse the cockpit, root, secondary, and creation conventions.

- A screen opened from Profile, Progress, or a mission is a push inside `(secondary)`. That group is already the one full-screen modal above the cockpit. Back pops inside the group. Close removes that one modal.
- A screen that must cover the cockpit from a tab is an `(app)` `fullScreenModal` via `secondaryPresentation`, and only when no other top-level route is open. Close flows the coordinator already owns through `navigation.ts`.
- Root routes (`login`, `bloom-preview`) render when `(app)` is not mounted. A root card pushed while `(app)` is mounted leaves the cockpit sheet presented. Do not put an in-app tool there, and do not hide that sheet with a pathname check in the root gate.

```tsx
// Wrong: root card. (app) stays mounted, so the sheet stays on top.
router.push('/listing-lab'); // file at app/listing-lab.tsx

// Right: push inside the secondary modal that is already above the sheet.
router.push('/listing-lab'); // file at app/(app)/(secondary)/listing-lab.tsx
```

Do not dismiss or remount the cockpit to reveal a page. Do not pop two modals in one update. That orphans a controller.

Add a router test in `app/src/lib/app-shell.test.ts` for the enter and back state. Native presentation still needs an iPhone 16 Pro check: the sheet, the tab bar, and that leaving does not orphan a controller. Say that explicitly.
